<p>
  A transformer oil breakdown voltage (BDV) result is only meaningful against the method that produced it. Utilities in the Gulf, Africa, India and elsewhere reference different standards, so the same oil sample can legitimately give different numbers depending on the test. This guide compares the main methods so you know which one your client expects and which tester you need.
</p>

<h2 id="what-bdv-tells">What BDV Actually Measures</h2>

<p>
  BDV is the voltage at which oil between two electrodes breaks down. It is mainly a screen for moisture and for particles and contamination in the oil. It does not tell you about oil chemistry such as acidity, which needs separate tests. A low BDV usually means water or dirt, and often the fix is filtering or drying the oil.
</p>

<h2 id="main-methods">The Main Methods</h2>

<div class="my-8 rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
  <div class="bg-[#0D1B2A] px-5 py-3 text-xs font-mono text-[#94a3b8] uppercase tracking-widest">BDV test methods - key differences</div>
  <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
    <thead>
      <tr class="bg-[#1B3A5B]">
        <th class="text-left px-5 py-3 text-blue-200 font-semibold text-xs">Method</th>
        <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 text-blue-200 font-semibold text-xs">Electrodes</th>
        <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 text-blue-200 font-semibold text-xs">Gap</th>
        <th class="text-left px-4 py-3 text-blue-200 font-semibold text-xs">Voltage rise</th>
