<Callout type="info" title="Always confirm against the current standard">
  Details such as electrode profile, stirring, rest time and the number of breakdowns averaged differ between editions and between methods. This table is a comparison guide, not a substitute for the standard your client specifies.
</Callout>

<h2 id="sensitivity">Sensitivity to Moisture</h2>

<p>
  Methods with the slower ramp and tighter gap, such as D1816, tend to be more sensitive to dissolved moisture and fibres than D877. Because of this, results from different methods should never be mixed in one trend. Pick the method your utility uses, stay with it, and record it on every report.
</p>

<h2 id="sampling">Sampling Matters as Much as the Tester</h2>

<p>
  A perfect tester gives a useless result on a bad sample. Draw the sample from the bottom drain valve after flushing it, use a clean dry container, avoid exposing it to air and test it soon after drawing. Rinse the test vessel with the oil being tested, fill slowly to avoid bubbles and allow the specified rest time before applying voltage. Our <a href={u('/blog/transformer-oil-bdv-test/')}>transformer oil BDV test guide</a> covers the procedure in full.
</p>

<h2 id="choosing-tester">Choosing a Tester</h2>

<p>
  The CIE/7000 series is built to IS 6792, with brass sphere electrodes and an adjustable gap of 2.5 mm or 4 mm. It spans eight output tiers from 0 to 50 kV up to 0 to 120 kV in manual and motorised versions, with the motorised type raising voltage at about 2 kV per second. That makes it a natural fit where IEC 60156 style testing at 2.5 mm is specified, but check that the test method your client names matches the equipment before ordering. See the <a href={u('/products/oil-test-sets/')}>oil test sets</a> range for the full table of models.
</p>

